finish('weighted-kamp-natural-number-finite-words',
 id='TCS-0484',title='Does the specified weighted temporal logic express every weighted FO sentence over natural numbers?',
 area='Automata and formal languages',criterion='characterization',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''For every finite alphabet Sigma and every closed weighted first-order expression E in the fragment defined below, with weights in the semiring (N,+,times,0,1), does there exist a weighted temporal expression H in the specified grammar such that E(w)=H(w,1) for every nonempty finite word w over Sigma? The weighted temporal grammar is $H ::= 0\mid Ls\mid\langle s\rangle H\mid s\,U_w\,H\mid H+H\mid\operatorname{if}(\varphi,H,H)$, where s is a finite conditional expression returning a natural-number constant and phi is an ordinary Boolean LTL formula with both future and past. Its exact semantics and the source weighted-FO grammar are given in Model & notation. No additional weighted operator may be introduced. This fixes the natural-number instance of the weighted Kamp question discussed in Gastin’s December 2025 slides.''',
 definitions=r'''Words w have positions 1,...,N, with N=|w|>=1. Boolean FO formulas theta use letter predicates a(x), equality, order, Boolean connectives, and quantification over positions. A step-FO expression s_F is a natural constant r or if(theta,s_F,s_F). Weighted FO expressions have grammar E ::= 0 | product_x s_F | sum_x E | E+E | if(theta,E,E). A sum or product ranges over every word position and binds x; conditionals select the indicated branch according to ordinary Boolean truth. Products are permitted only over step expressions, not arbitrary weighted expressions. E is closed when all position variables are bound.

Boolean LTL guards phi use true, letter tests, negation, disjunction, strong next X, strong previous Y, until U, and since S. X phi is false at the last position and Y phi false at the first. At i, phi U psi holds if some k>=i satisfies psi and every j with i<=j<k satisfies phi; since is the reverse-time counterpart. Guards are evaluated on the entire word, at the indicated position. A step-temporal expression s is a natural constant or if(phi,s,s).

Write s(w,i) for its value. The weighted semantics is: 0 has value zero; addition is pointwise ordinary addition; conditionals select one branch. (Ls)(w,i)=s(w,i) if i=N and zero otherwise. (<s>H)(w,i)=s(w,i) H(w,i+1) if i<N and zero otherwise. Finally, $(s\,U_w\,H)(w,i)=\sum_{k=i}^{N}(\prod_{j=i}^{k-1}s(w,j))H(w,k)$, with empty product one. This U_w is a numerical summation operator, distinct from Boolean until inside a guard. A temporal expression denotes the function w -> H(w,1). Arbitrary multiplication of weighted expressions and weighted past operators are absent from the grammar. All expressions are finite, with any fixed natural constants allowed; equality is exact on every word, not approximation or equality only of nonzero supports.''',
 answer_criterion='Translate every closed expression of the specified weighted FO fragment into the specified weighted temporal grammar, or give an expression E and prove that no temporal expression in that grammar agrees with it on all nonempty finite words. It is enough for a negative answer to use one finite alphabet. Changing the semiring or adding a new weighted operator does not answer this fixed question. Agreement only on the underlying Boolean language is insufficient.',
 context=[
  paragraph('Classical first-order logic and temporal logic can describe the same finite-word languages despite using different means of referring to positions. Weighted expressions ask for an exact number associated with a word. A position quantifier can now add contributions from many witnesses, rather than merely asserting that a witness exists. The question is whether a temporal expression can reproduce those totals without explicit position variables.'),
  paragraph('The source FO fragment deliberately restricts products to step expressions. Permitting products of arbitrary weighted sums would define a stronger language and change the question. Similarly, weighted until has a step expression on the left: it accumulates products along an interval and adds the possible endpoint contributions. These restrictions are part of the mathematical target, not choices left to a proposed translation.'),
  paragraph('For intuition, a weighted FO sentence can count occurrences of factors belonging to a chosen FO-definable language L. It sums one over pairs x<=y whenever the substring between those positions belongs to L. A temporal formulation must reproduce the same multiplicity, including overlapping occurrences. Ordinary Boolean temporal tests can decide many properties of prefixes and suffixes, but that alone does not give a way to sum over two independently chosen endpoints.', 'slides'),
  paragraph('The February 2025 seminar leaves the appropriate weighted LTL formulation unspecified. Gastin’s later slides give an explicit grammar and semantics, establish equivalences in unambiguous and finite-ambiguity fragments, and mark the full weighted-FO/weighted-LTL equivalence with a question mark. This card uses that later formulation and fixes the natural-number semiring. Thus an answer concerns a particular expressive-power claim, rather than whether some future language definition would be considered adequate.', 'seminar')],
 why='This asks whether the classical equivalence between variable-based and temporal descriptions survives exact quantitative aggregation. It could identify a basic limit of temporal specification languages and clarify which operations are needed to describe the counts and weights recognized by aperiodic automata.',
 importance=dict(score=78,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='A quantitative analogue of a central expressiveness theorem, connecting finite-word logic with aperiodic weighted automata. The fixed natural-number setting makes the question concrete while retaining the difficulty of counting multiple witnesses.'),
 progress=[
  step('2019','Droste and Gastin characterize the relevant weighted FO fragment using aperiodic polynomially ambiguous weighted automata, with finer correspondences when first-order and binary sums are removed.','automata'),
  step('2025-02','The Dagstuhl problem discusses extending the classical star-free, aperiodic, FO, and LTL equivalences to weighted languages; the temporal-language definition is still part of its research direction.','seminar'),
  step('2025-12-16','Gastin gives explicit weighted LTL syntax and semantics. Slides 25–28 establish restricted-fragment equivalences and leave the full weighted FO versus weighted LTL correspondence open; slide 30 highlights counting factors as a test example.','slides')],
 references=[
  ref('slides','Weighted first-order logic, weighted aperiodic automata, and more','Paul Gastin',2025,'https://quantformal-2025.vercel.app/slides/quantformal-2025-paul-gastin.pdf','QuantFormal, 16 December 2025; slides 6–7 (weighted FO), 22–23 (weighted LTL), 25–30 (known fragments and open correspondence); numbering starts at 1'),
  ref('seminar','Semirings in Databases, Automata, and Logic (Dagstuhl Seminar 25081)','Manfred Droste; Paul Gastin',2025,'https://doi.org/10.4230/DagRep.15.2.89','Section 4.4, Weighted First-Order Definable Languages'),
  ref('automata','Aperiodic Weighted Automata and Weighted First-Order Logic','Manfred Droste; Paul Gastin',2019,'https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.MFCS.2019.76','Weighted FO fragment and its ambiguity-sensitive automata characterization')],
 status='source_open',status_note='The unrestricted correspondence is marked open in the December 2025 primary slides. This card fixes their temporal syntax and restricts to the natural-number semiring; it does not claim all alternative weighted LTL definitions are equivalent. Searches through 10 September 2026 did not locate a resolution of this exact instance.',
 formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
